  "narrative": "The enrichment analysis reveals a striking metabolic signature dominated by **Tryptophan metabolism (KEGG map00380)** — the top pathway with an FDR of 0.0 and a fold-enrichment of 600.5, with six of the ten input metabolites matching. Membership of the key drivers — L-Tryptophan (C00078), Serotonin (C00780), and Melatonin (C01598) — is confirmed across KEGG and Reactome.\n\nDirectional analysis via the KEGG reaction graph shows that **L-Tryptophan is a verified precursor of Serotonin** (path: C00078 → C00643 → C00780, 2 hops), and **Serotonin is a verified upstream precursor of Melatonin** (path: C00780 → C00978 → C01598, 2 hops). These three metabolites form a coherent, validated biosynthetic chain. The remaining matched compounds — Tryptamine (C00398), 3-Hydroxyanthranilic acid (C00632), and Indoleacetaldehyde (C00637) — represent parallel branch points within the same pathway, covering both the kynurenine and serotonin/melatonin arms of tryptophan catabolism.\n\nThe biological significance is considerable. Elevated tryptophan metabolism along the serotonin–melatonin axis suggests either enhanced neurochemical flux — consistent with serotonergic or circadian signaling activation — or a shift in substrate allocation away from the kynurenine arm, which has immunomodulatory and oxidative-stress implications. The presence of 4-Hydroxy-L-glutamic acid (C05947) hints at broader amino-acid remoding. Notably, Molybdenum (C00150), a cofactor for xanthine oxidase and sulfite oxidase, did not enrich in any pathway, suggesting its differential abundance may reflect an independent metabolic perturbation rather than a co-regulatory effect within the tryptophan network.\n\n**In summary**, this dataset most strongly implicates dysregulated **Tryptophan metabolism**, driven by coordinated changes in L-Tryptophan, Serotonin, and Melatonin, with verified upstream-to-downstream relationships connecting these three metabolites along the established serotonin–melatonin 